/* Shared, cross-window RCSS for the modern theme: generic (non-positional) rules reused by more
   than one migrated window, plus the anchor/center/stretch layout utility classes every window
   uses. Every migrated window's <head> links this file first, then its own per-window positional
   .rcss.

   Colors/borders/shadows below are named against this theme's design-token table -- keep the
   trailing token-name comment next to each value; that table is the source of truth, this file is
   its first application.

   Depth primitives (.modern-frame/.modern-panel/.modern-inset, .btn/.checkbox-box below) simulate
   raised/recessed physical surfaces without box-shadow, which this engine's RenderInterface can't
   composite (confirmed broken -- see the token doc). The substitute is per-side border-color
   bevels (RmlUi supports border-top/right/bottom/left-color as independent properties) plus
   `decorator: linear-gradient(...)`, which IS a real working decorator here (proven by
   main_frame.rcss's gauge fills before this). Light source is fixed upper-left throughout: a
   RAISED surface gets a lighter top/left border and a lighter gradient corner there, darker
   bottom/right; a RECESSED surface inverts both. Never mix the two directions on one element. */

body {
	width: 100%;
	height: 100%;
	margin: 0;
	pointer-events: none;
}

/* Full-screen dim overlay for modal-style windows (SysMenuWin/OptionWin). */
#backdrop {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	top: 0;
	width: 100%;
	height: 100%;
	background-color: token(surface-0);
	pointer-events: auto;
}

.checkbox-row {
	position: absolute;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
}
/* Matches login.rcss's own checkbox exactly (that file can't reference this rule -- it doesn't
   link base.rcss, see login.rcss's own note -- so the values are duplicated by hand; keep the two
   in sync if either changes). A real gradient + bevel border. */
.checkbox-box {
	display: inline-block;
	width: 16dp;
	height: 16dp;
	background-color: #0a0d10;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #1a2027, #0a0d10);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-metal);
	border-top-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 180);
	border-radius: token(radius-sm)dp;
}
.checkbox-box.checked {
	background-color: #6b5220;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, token(accent-gold), #6b5220); /* bright stop = accent-gold exactly; dark stop is a derived shade, no token */
	border-color: token(accent-gold);
}
.checkbox-label {
	position: absolute;
	left: 21dp;
	top: 1dp;
	white-space: nowrap;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
}

/* Flat -- no gradient fill/bevel -- but the border carries a faint steel tint at rest
   that brightens into a real steel glow on hover. Gray surface stays the base material; steel is
   the accent, not the structure -- gold is reserved for text emphasis/item-skill highlights, not
   general chrome. */
.btn {
	position: absolute;
	width: 108dp;
	height: 30dp;
	text-align: center;
	vertical-align: middle;
	line-height: 30dp;
	cursor: pointer;
	color: token(text-body);
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
	background-color: #262b30;
	border: 1dp rgba(118, 136, 154, 90); /* faint accent-steel */
	border-radius: token(radius-md)dp;
	pointer-events: auto;
}
.btn:hover {
	background-color: #333a41;
	border-color: token(accent-steel-bright);
}
.btn:active {
	/* Native :active pseudo-class, unverified against this branch's own mouse-input routing
	   (RmlUi's default SDL translation is bypassed here for unrelated reasons). Degrades
	   gracefully if it never fires. border-color is accent-crimson's exact rgb at reduced alpha --
	   can't express that as token(accent-crimson) directly (token substitution is plain text, it
	   can't be blended with a different alpha at the call site), so it stays a literal here. */
	background-color: #16191c;
	border-color: rgba(165, 35, 54, 130); /* accent-crimson @ 130 alpha */
}
.btn.disabled {
	pointer-events: none;
	background-color: #1e2124;
	color: token(semantic-neutral);
	border-color: rgba(122, 133, 145, 60); /* border-metal's rgb @ 60 alpha, stays neutral when disabled, no gold */
	opacity: 0.55;
	cursor: default;
}

/* Opt-in primary/hero variant -- apply alongside .btn (class="btn btn-ok") for a
   window's one confirm-style action; plain .btn above stays flat/neutral, unaffected, still the
   right choice for a list of equal-weight actions (sys_menu.rcss's four stacked menu buttons).
   Exact same accent-crimson gradient/bevel as login.rcss's own .btn (that file has its own copy,
   same reason as .checkbox-box above -- it doesn't link base.rcss). Overrides only color
   properties, not layout -- .btn's own position/size/border-width apply underneath. Must stay
   textually after .btn/.btn:hover/.btn:active above (equal specificity once combined with .btn on
   the same element, source order decides). */
.btn-ok {
	background-color: #430e17;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, token(accent-crimson-bright), token(accent-crimson) 35%, #6c1520 70%, #430e17); /* bright/base stops are real tokens; the two darker stops are derived shades with no token of their own */
	border-color: #0a0706;
	border-top-color: rgba(211, 74, 94, 115); /* accent-crimson-bright's rgb @ 115 alpha */
}
.btn-ok:hover {
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #e05f72, token(accent-crimson-bright) 35%, #8c1e2c 70%, #571219); /* #e05f72 is a brighter-than-token hover-only highlight, no token */
	border-color: #e05f72;
}
.btn-ok:active {
	border-top-color: rgba(165, 35, 54, 65); /* accent-crimson's rgb @ 65 alpha */
	border-bottom-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 200);
}
.btn-ok.disabled {
	/* .btn.disabled's own flat neutral background-color/border-color/opacity already apply
	   (two-class selector, same specificity as this rule, doesn't touch decorator either way).
	   This rule's own job is just resetting decorator to none -- two classes here beats .btn-ok's
	   one-class decorator declaration on specificity alone, not source order. Without it, the
	   crimson gradient decorator paints over/hides the disabled neutral fill entirely, since
	   decorators composite on top of background-color. */
	decorator: none;
}

/* Reusable window-layering primitives. Intended usage: apply .modern-frame to the outer
   positioned panel element itself (it keeps that element's own position/size), then add ONE
   decorative child div with .modern-panel, inset a few px on all sides, as the FIRST child (so it
   paints behind real content, same convention as .layout-anchor/.panel-header) -- the small
   reveal between the frame's inner edge and the panel's own border is deliberate, it's the
   frame's own material showing as a third visual band. Real content stays direct children of the
   .modern-frame element at their original positions -- do NOT nest content inside .modern-panel,
   its inset would shift every child's effective position.

   There's no CSS variable mechanism on this engine (confirmed), so a shared class can only carry
   *structural* properties
   (border-width, the bevel/gradient mechanism) portably; a window's own specific palette (login's
   warm bronze/crimson, say) still has to come from somewhere. The pattern that actually closes
   that gap, proven by .btn/.btn-ok above: keep .modern-frame/.modern-panel as generic gray
   defaults, and add a *named palette variant* modifier class per distinct look a window
   contributes (e.g. .modern-frame-crimson below, extracted from login.rcss's own #panel) instead
   of leaving every window to hand-roll its own copy of the whole bevel mechanism from scratch.
   Apply both classes together (class="modern-frame modern-frame-crimson"); the variant only
   overrides color/decorator properties, .modern-frame's own border-width/mechanism still applies
   underneath. Window-specific structural values (width, height, border-radius, the exact inset
   used for a "leaves room for a header above" child panel) stay the consuming window's own
   responsibility either way -- these classes were never meant to carry position/size, see the
   original usage note above. */
.modern-frame {
	background-color: rgba(29, 35, 43, 190); /* surface-2's rgb @ 190 alpha -- can't use token(surface-2) directly, it has no alpha channel of its own */
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(45, 53, 63, 190) 0%, rgba(29, 35, 43, 190) 40%, rgba(14, 17, 21, 190) 100%);
	border-width: 2dp;
	border-top-color: rgba(170, 184, 198, 160); /* accent-steel-bright's rgb @ 160 alpha: bevel-light, brighter than the fill for a real edge-catch */
	border-left-color: rgba(170, 184, 198, 160);
	border-bottom-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 190); /* bevel-dark */
	border-right-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 190);
}
.modern-panel {
	position: absolute;
	left: 4dp;
	right: 4dp;
	top: 4dp;
	bottom: 4dp;
	pointer-events: none;
	background-color: token(surface-1);
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(20, 24, 29, 180) 0%, rgba(11, 13, 16, 180) 100%);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-top-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 140); /* bevel-dark-inset: recessed relative to the frame */
	border-left-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 140);
	border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 20); /* bevel-light-inset */
	border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 20);
	border-radius: 8dp;
}

/* Cool gray/metallic near-black palette variant -- extracted from login.rcss's own
   #panel/.login-content, the first real consumer of these primitives. Apply alongside
   .modern-frame/.modern-panel (class="modern-frame modern-frame-crimson"), not instead of them.
   Despite the name this isn't crimson itself (that's accent-crimson, reserved for a primary
   button/banner) -- it's the dark neutral
   *base* those accent colors and the dialog's own title text sit on top of, kept under its
   original "-crimson" name for now even though the frame material itself is no longer warm --
   title/label text and the checkbox stay warm/gold deliberately (general body text is cool,
   see tokens.ini's text-body); only the frame/panel material itself goes cool gray/metallic. */
.modern-frame-crimson {
	background-color: #171b20;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #232a32, #171b20 28%, #101317);
	border-top-color: rgba(150, 161, 172, 90); /* border-strong's rgb @ 90 alpha: cool steel highlight catching the top edge */
	border-left-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 160);
	border-right-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 160);
	border-bottom-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 220);
}
.modern-panel-crimson {
	background-color: #101316;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #171b1f, #101316 55%, #0b0d0f);
	border-top-color: rgba(150, 161, 172, 45); /* border-strong's rgb @ 45 alpha */
	border-bottom-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 240);
	border-left-color: #08080a;
	border-right-color: #08080a;
}

/* Thin accent line just inside .modern-frame -- border only, no fill, so the frame's own gradient
   still shows through it. Extracted from login.rcss's .frame-accent; apply as a
   direct child of .modern-frame, inset a few px more than .modern-panel so .modern-panel's own
   position/size stays exactly as documented above -- this is a fourth visual band, not a
   replacement for the frame/panel pair. Position/size (left/right/top/bottom/border-radius) stay
   the consuming window's own responsibility, same as .modern-frame/.modern-panel themselves. */
.modern-frame-accent {
	position: absolute;
	pointer-events: none;
	border-width: 1dp;
}
.modern-frame-accent-crimson {
	border-color: rgba(122, 133, 145, 166); /* border-metal's rgb @ 166 alpha, cool steel, was warm bronze */
	border-top-color: token(accent-steel-bright);
	border-bottom-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 200);
}

/* Border-only recessed bevel, no fill -- for an element with legacy-drawn content painted
   underneath it (RmlUi always composites last, so any fill here would hide that content; see
   login.rcss's .input-frame comment for a concrete example). Gives the "sunk into the panel"
   read from the border bevel alone. */
.modern-inset {
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-top-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 190); /* bevel-dark-inset */
	border-left-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 190);
	border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 35); /* bevel-light-inset */
	border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 35);
}
.modern-inset.focused {
	border-top-color: token(accent-steel);
	border-left-color: rgba(118, 136, 154, 170); /* accent-steel's rgb @ 170 alpha */
}

/* Reusable "fading crimson banner" -- a soft ambient radial glow meant to sit behind a window's
   title text (gold core cooling to a crimson ring, fading to transparent before the edge so it
   reads as a glow, not a hard-edged shape). Extracted from login.rcss's own .header-glow, the
   first real consumer -- a true radial falloff since RenderInterface_SDL_GPU implements
   CompileShader/RenderShader for the gradient family. Position/size (left/right/top/height) stay
   the consuming window's own responsibility, same convention as .modern-frame/.modern-panel above
   -- apply alongside a window-specific sizing class (class="title-glow header-glow", say), and
   place it as a sibling BEFORE the title text it sits behind (RmlUi paints earlier siblings
   first). Center-stop alpha (130) and fade-out point (90%) are tuned low/late so the gold core
   stays soft instead of reading as a bold solid patch -- see login.rcss's own comment for the
   fuller tuning history if this ever needs revisiting. */
.title-glow {
	position: absolute;
	pointer-events: none;
	decorator: radial-gradient(ellipse, rgba(255, 222, 150, 130), rgba(165, 35, 54, 140) 55%, transparent 90%);
}

.hidden {
	display: none;
}

/* Layout primitives -- identical to
   themes/legacy/base.rcss's copy; pure layout, nothing theme-specific to vary. */

.anchor-top-left {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	top: 0;
}
.anchor-top-right {
	position: absolute;
	right: 0;
	top: 0;
}
.anchor-bottom-left {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	bottom: 0;
}
.anchor-bottom-right {
	position: absolute;
	right: 0;
	bottom: 0;
}

.center-x {
	position: absolute;
	left: 50%;
	transform: translateX(-50%);
}
.center-y {
	position: absolute;
	top: 50%;
	transform: translateY(-50%);
}
.center-both {
	position: absolute;
	left: 50%;
	top: 50%;
	transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}

.stretch-x {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	right: 0;
}
.stretch-y {
	position: absolute;
	top: 0;
	bottom: 0;
}
.stretch-both {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	right: 0;
	top: 0;
	bottom: 0;
}

/* Invisible marker read by C++ via Element::GetAbsoluteOffset() -- see themes/legacy/base.rcss's
   identical comment for the full mechanism. */
.layout-anchor {
	position: absolute;
	visibility: hidden;
	width: 0;
	height: 0;
	pointer-events: none;
}
